      "body": "The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms without smuggling in induction if framed as a critical preference based on verisimilitude rather than probabilistic inference. The strongest case posits that while corroboration reports past survival, the rational choice to act on the best-tested theory is a pragmatic decision aimed at maximizing truth-likeness (verisimilitude) in an evolutionary process of conjecture and refutation. This view survives because it distinguishes between the logical impossibility of proving a theory true (induction) and the rational necessity of choosing the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification as the one currently closest to the truth. Accepting this costs the scientist any claim to certainty or a quantifiable probability of success; it requires accepting that science progresses not by accumulating probable truths, but by eliminating error, leaving 'verisimilitude' as a regulative ideal rather than a measurable metric, and acknowledging that even the best-corroborated theory may be false tomorrow."
